CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute, severe autonomic crisis, including intractable vomiting, marked tachycardia (HR 115), and severe hyperventilation resulting directly from profound psychological trauma (spiritual abuse trigger). Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was critical to perform a targeted abdominal examination, definitively exclude an acute surgical abdomen or severe organic pathology, and administer parenteral antiemetics to halt the vomiting and prevent severe dehydration. Delaying assessment until the morning would have perpetuated severe physical distress and risked significant metabolic derangement, fulfilling the criteria for urgent intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular ondansetron 4mg to immediately halt the intractable vomiting. Guided the patient through a '5-4-3-2-1' sensory grounding exercise to correct the hyperventilation and slow the heart rate. Advised the usual GP to arrange narrative exposure testing confirming complex PTSD etiology linked to high-control environments.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the acute panic and nausea safely, implement the "cold-dive reflex": hold an ice pack or a bag of frozen vegetables against the eyes and bridge of the nose for 30 seconds during a trigger response; this immediately forces the parasympathetic nervous system to slow the heart rate and override the nausea. To combat the deconstruction existential vacuum, establish a "secular anchoring ritual": dedicate 10 minutes every morning to watering a houseplant or making a specific type of tea; creating a new, completely non-dogmatic daily routine provides a safe, controllable framework for the day.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a specialized trauma therapist experienced in religious trauma syndrome and high-control group recovery.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48 hours for a comprehensive psychological assessment and ongoing support coordination.

